From 6006d8752ca700f2ffc3fe550e7dbb878c807101 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 12:28:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2024/2064] VideoPlayer: pack the separately coded views of an
 MV-HEVC stream side by side

Apple "spatial video" and other MV-HEVC files code the two eyes as two layers
of one HEVC bitstream rather than packing them into one frame. ffmpeg decodes
both from 7.1 onwards, but only when asked, and it hands them back as separate
frames sharing a pts. Kodi has no representation for that - every renderer
expects one frame holding both eyes - so such a file played as a flat 2D clip
of the base view.

It does already handle a full side by side frame correctly, cropping an eye in
CBaseRenderer::ManageRenderArea() and undoing the doubled aspect in
GetPerEyeAspectRatio(). So rather than teach the renderers about separate
views, pack the views in the decoder and hand the renderers what they already
understand.

The demuxer flags such a stream, which is the AVStereo3D unspecified type with
both views present, and reports the mode the decoder is going to produce. That
tag is what selects a 3D display mode before the first frame is decoded, so it
has to describe the packed frame, not the coded layout. The new multiview flag
travels beside it so the decoder knows the packing is still owed.

Both decoders then set the hevc view_ids option to decode every view, and gain
a second buffer source feeding hstack ahead of whatever the chain already held.
hstack is framesync based and pairs frames by pts, which is exactly what the
decoder gives the two views of an access unit, so a dropped or missing view
reuses the previous one for that timestamp rather than swapping the eyes for
the rest of the stream. Frames are routed to a source by AV_FRAME_DATA_VIEW_ID,
with the first view seen taking the left half; if the bitstream says that view
is the right eye the reported mode becomes right_left rather than the graph
being rewired.

No hardware accelerator decodes the dependent layer, so multiview has to force
software decoding in both paths. A hwaccel is not a decoder in its own right -
the ordinary decoder offers it as an extra pixel format - so turning one down
means turning down that format in get_format(), which is where
DVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME now does it, having also not created a hardware device
context for one to attach to. Left to itself it picked drm_prime on a Pi and
handed hstack frames whose contents live on the GPU, which wedged playback.
DVDVideoCodecFFmpeg declines to attach an IHardwareDecoder for the same reason,
which would otherwise skip the filter graph entirely.

Neither decoder overrides the thread type for multiview: ffmpeg supports
frame threading for it, and measured on a four core Pi 4 against a six
slice H.264 MVC stream, 1000 frames decode in 13.9s with it against 16.1s
with slice threading alone.

On a Pi 4 playing a 1080p23.98 Blu-ray MVC rip that is the difference between
unwatchable and correct. Before, the decoder produced frames at half rate, one
core of four busy, video falling steadily behind audio - presenting 73 frames
late with the late frame count climbing past 7000 in seconds, and the player
periodically seeking to catch up. After, two cores are busy, the late frame count
sits at 11 and does not grow over a 75 second run, and nothing resyncs.

Filter output on the DRMPRIME path is still allocated through GetBuffer(), so
the packed frame lands in a DMA buffer and direct to plane scanout is
unaffected. That needs a second buffer pool: a pool serves exactly the one size
it was configured with, and the packed frame is twice as wide as the decoder's
own frames, so the request fell through to the only registered pool factory and
came back as system memory that GetBuffer() could not use. Register another dma
pool on that miss - an unconfigured one takes the size of the first request it
sees - and release the buffer that came from the wrong pool rather than leaking
it. The graph is kept up for the whole stream, since it is now load bearing
rather than an optional deinterlace, and if it cannot be built the stream is
downgraded to mono so the base view plays as 2D rather than the renderer
cropping half of a single view.

Kodi accepts a system ffmpeg from 7.0, where none of view_ids,
AV_STEREO3D_UNSPEC or AV_FRAME_DATA_VIEW_ID exist, so the multiview paths are
guarded on 7.1. If the option is rejected at runtime the stream is reported as
mono for the same reason.

Verified with a 1920x1080 30fps MV-HEVC sample, on the software path and on a
Pi 4 through DVDVideoCodecDRMPRIME: the demuxer reports the stereo metadata,
the graph configures as two 1920x1080 sources into hstack out to 3840x1080, the
decoder pairs view 0 as the left eye, dma buffers are allocated at both sizes,
and RenderManager is configured for 3840x1080 at 30fps.

(cherry picked from commit 7977e6170ddb71ff8ea888bdde4d81cd855aabe7)
